0	130	Corina Crețu  (born 24 June 1967) is Consul General, head of the Consulate General of Romania in Thessaloniki, Hellenic Republic.

130	195	Crețu is a former European Commissioner for Cohesion and Reforms.
196	302	and Member of the European Parliament (sitting with the Progressive Alliance of Socialists and Democrats).
303	400	Between June 2014 and October 2014, she served as a Vice-President of the European Parliament.



400	510	Political career
Crețu studied at the Academy of Economic Studies, Faculty of Cybernetics, graduating in 1989.
511	584	She spent a year working as an economist at a factory in Blaj until 1990.
585	817	She then worked as a journalist and political commentator between 1990 and 1992 for newspapers Azi, Curierul Național, and Cronica Română before joining the Spokesperson's office of the Cabinet of Pre
817	894	In 1996, she became a member of the Romanian Social Democratic Party (PDSR).

894	1082	Between 2000 and 2004, Crețu was Presidential Advisor, Presidential Spokesperson and Head of the Public Communication Department during Ion Iliescu's second mandate as Romanian president.

1082	1174	In 2000 she was elected Deputy in Romania's Parliament and, in 2004, to the Romanian Senate.
1175	1321	As a Senator, she sat on the Foreign Policy Committee, and was a full member of the Romanian Delegation to the Parliamentary Assembly of the OSCE.
1322	1477	In January 2005, at the invitation of the Jordanian Government, she conducted a training seminar at Amman for appointees to spokesperson positions in Iraq.
1478	1628	Crețu was also an OSCE observer to the parliamentary election of March 2005 in Moldova and to the general election of 2006 in Bosnia and Herzegovina.

1628	1734	In 2005, Crețu was appointed a member of the Romanian parliamentary delegation to the European Parliament.
1735	1980	She was elected Member of the European Parliament (MEP), sitting with the Progressive Alliance of Socialists and Democrats on January 1, 2007 following the accession of Romania to the European Union, 
1980	2067	In 2013, she was elected vice-president of the Romanian Social Democratic Party (PSD).

2067	2203	Crețu announced on 17 January 2019 that she would be a candidate in the European Parliament election on behalf of the party Pro Romania.
2204	2267	She was at the second position in the list after Victor Ponta.

2267	2322	She joined Victor Ponta's PRO Romania in March 2019.



2322	2442	Personal life
Her father, Traian Crețu (1937–1995), was Professor of Physics at the Politehnica University of Bucharest.
2443	2558	Her mother, Verginia Crețu is a child development psychologist and was a professor at the University of Bucharest.

2558	2666	In 2012, Crețu married Ovidiu Rogoz, a Romanian businessman, at the New Church of St Spyridon in Bucharest.

